# Non-null safety

This project is checked with Eclipse JDT null analysis in the IDE. Avoid warnings such as unchecked conversion to `@Nonnull`.

## Optional → instance methods

Do not pass `Optional.map` a method reference when the receiver type is treated as `@Nonnull` (e.g. `Kingdom::getDisplayName`). The mapper argument is not proven non-null to the analyser.

```java
// ❌ BAD — method reference on Optional value
kingdomService.getKingdom(id)
    .map(Kingdom::getDisplayName)
    .orElse(id);

// ✅ GOOD — guard before calling instance methods
Optional<Kingdom> kingdom = kingdomService.getKingdom(id);
String displayName = kingdom.isPresent() ? kingdom.get().getDisplayName() : id;

// ✅ GOOD — explicit lambda when the analyser accepts it
kingdomService.getKingdom(id)
    .map(kingdom -> kingdom.getDisplayName())
    .orElse(id);
```

Prefer `isPresent()` / `isEmpty()` guards when the warning persists.

## General habits

- Narrow `Optional` with `isPresent()` before `get()`, or use `orElse` / `orElseGet` with a safe default.
- Do not call `Optional.get()` without a prior empty check.
- After editing Java, run `ReadLints` on touched files and fix null-safety warnings before finishing.
